1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,450
A wall fifty meters high.

2
00:00:01,510 --> 00:00:06,102
For a hundred years, nothing had ever breached it — not an army, not a siege,

3
00:00:06,162 --> 00:00:06,646
not once.

4
00:00:06,706 --> 00:00:09,788
And then one morning, fingers appeared over the top.

5
00:00:09,848 --> 00:00:14,017
Human-shaped fingers, cracked and steaming, each one four meters wide.

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,034
But here's the strange part, and it's the part almost nobody talks about.

7
00:00:28,094 --> 00:00:29,940
Cut off one of those massive arms,

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,371
and a single kick sends it bouncing across the mud like an empty cardboard box.

9
00:00:34,431 --> 00:00:36,950
A sixty-meter monster, lighter than styrofoam.

10
00:00:37,010 --> 00:00:41,268
That's where today's story starts — not with how terrifying this creature is,

11
00:00:41,328 --> 00:00:42,390
but with a question.

12
00:00:42,450 --> 00:00:44,409
Why did this giant have to be light?

13
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:53,157
The answer was written three hundred and eighty years before the manga ever existed.

14
00:00:53,217 --> 00:00:54,213
The year is 1638.

15
00:00:54,273 --> 00:00:58,125
Galileo Galilei, old and going blind, publishes his final book.

16
00:00:58,185 --> 00:01:01,976
And buried in it is a warning about giants: they cannot exist.

17
00:01:02,036 --> 00:01:04,274
Scale a body up far enough, he wrote,

18
00:01:04,334 --> 00:01:06,882
and it will collapse under its own weight.

19
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:14,660
The logic is simple enough to do on your fingers.

20
00:01:14,720 --> 00:01:15,659
Double the height,

21
00:01:15,719 --> 00:01:20,654
and the bone's cross-section — the part that actually carries the load — grows four times.

22
00:01:20,714 --> 00:01:22,874
But the body's weight grows eight times.

23
00:01:22,934 --> 00:01:24,150
Now scale it ten times.

24
00:01:24,210 --> 00:01:26,315
The bone gets a hundred times stronger,

25
00:01:26,375 --> 00:01:28,812
while the body gets a thousand times heavier.

26
00:01:28,872 --> 00:01:30,588
Strength scales with the square.

27
00:01:30,648 --> 00:01:32,142
Weight scales with the cube.

28
00:01:32,202 --> 00:01:35,639
And the wider that gap grows, the less a body can carry itself.

29
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,540
So what happens to a fifteen-meter titan built at human density?

30
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:48,722
Watch the first step.

31
00:01:48,782 --> 00:01:51,140
The instant its weight shifts onto one leg,

32
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:56,147
the stress inside that thigh bone climbs past the limit that bone can physically survive.

33
00:01:56,207 --> 00:01:57,553
It isn't walking anymore.

34
00:01:57,613 --> 00:01:58,453
It's collapsing.

35
00:01:58,513 --> 00:01:59,635
The geneticist J.B.S.

36
00:01:59,695 --> 00:02:04,585
Haldane summed this up in a single line that has never been improved on: for every size,

37
00:02:04,645 --> 00:02:05,935
there is a fitting form.

38
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:09,942
And the numbers only make it worse.

39
00:02:10,002 --> 00:02:15,776
A fifteen-meter titan at human density weighs about forty-eight tons — the same as a main battle tank.

40
00:02:15,836 --> 00:02:18,122
But look at how a tank carries that load.

41
00:02:18,182 --> 00:02:21,668
Two long tracks, spreading the weight across meters of ground.

42
00:02:21,728 --> 00:02:23,327
The titan has two footprints.

43
00:02:23,387 --> 00:02:25,157
That's the entire contact patch.

44
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:34,440
But something fails before the bones ever do.

45
00:02:34,500 --> 00:02:34,995
Body heat.

46
00:02:35,055 --> 00:02:36,495
Heat is produced by volume,

47
00:02:36,555 --> 00:02:40,551
by every cell in the body burning fuel — and volume scales with the cube.

48
00:02:40,611 --> 00:02:41,995
Heat escapes through skin,

49
00:02:42,055 --> 00:02:45,329
through surface area — and that scales only with the square.

50
00:02:45,389 --> 00:02:47,884
So the bigger you get, the more heat you make,

51
00:02:47,944 --> 00:02:49,773
and the narrower the exit becomes.

52
00:02:49,833 --> 00:02:50,773
The furnace grows.

53
00:02:50,833 --> 00:02:51,884
The chimney doesn't.

54
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,309
Run the numbers and the result is merciless.

55
00:03:06,369 --> 00:03:16,646
A fifteen-meter titan with a human metabolic rate would drive its internal temperature past two hundred degrees Celsius — roughly three times the point where the proteins in a body cook solid.

56
00:03:16,706 --> 00:03:18,261
It would never reach an enemy.

57
00:03:18,321 --> 00:03:20,738
It would never even finish crossing the field.

58
00:03:20,798 --> 00:03:23,807
It would cook, standing there, in heat of its own making.

59
00:03:23,867 --> 00:03:26,391
Snap your bones walking, or boil standing still.

60
00:03:26,451 --> 00:03:29,406
Physics offered exactly two deaths, and no third option.

61
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:38,448
But the author knew about every bit of this.

62
00:03:38,508 --> 00:03:42,722
He knew — and he had an answer ready long before anyone asked the question.

63
00:03:42,782 --> 00:03:46,198
In the story, a researcher is examining a severed titan limb,

64
00:03:46,258 --> 00:03:47,908
expecting tons of dead weight,

65
00:03:47,968 --> 00:03:51,783
and instead shouts a single line: it's strange — it's far too light!

66
00:03:51,843 --> 00:03:54,177
That one sentence, thrown away in a panel,

67
00:03:54,237 --> 00:03:57,653
quietly solves every physics problem we just watched pile up.

68
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:03,655
Put titan tissue under a microscope and the answer is right there.

69
00:04:03,715 --> 00:04:07,427
It isn't packed solid with muscle at all — it's a porous structure,

70
00:04:07,487 --> 00:04:10,016
riddled with air pockets, walls thin as paper.

71
00:04:10,076 --> 00:04:12,156
It's the same trick used by styrofoam.

72
00:04:12,216 --> 00:04:13,225
By a loaf of bread.

73
00:04:13,285 --> 00:04:16,940
And by the spongy bone sitting inside your own skeleton right now.

74
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:18,967
Keep the volume, delete the density.

75
00:04:19,027 --> 00:04:22,176
Nature worked this out hundreds of millions of years ago.

76
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:33,965
Let's feel the scale for a second.

77
00:04:34,025 --> 00:04:39,504
A sixty-meter colossal titan has roughly the volume of eighteen blue whales stacked together.

78
00:04:39,564 --> 00:04:40,517
At human density,

79
00:04:40,577 --> 00:04:44,686
that is thousands of tons — a number that simply cannot stand on legs.

80
00:04:44,746 --> 00:04:47,783
And yet in the story, this thing climbs onto a wall.

81
00:04:47,843 --> 00:04:48,319
It walks.

82
00:04:48,379 --> 00:04:48,855
It kicks.

83
00:04:48,915 --> 00:04:50,999
Which tells you the density was cut.

84
00:04:51,059 --> 00:04:52,190
And cut drastically.

85
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:58,162
And here the twist flips one more time.

86
00:04:58,222 --> 00:04:59,986
Because light doesn't mean weak.

87
00:05:00,046 --> 00:05:02,892
Destructive power isn't mass — it's kinetic energy.

88
00:05:02,952 --> 00:05:05,114
Mass, times velocity squared, over two.

89
00:05:05,174 --> 00:05:08,989
Halve the mass but double the speed, and the damage doubles with it.

90
00:05:09,049 --> 00:05:09,843
On top of that,

91
00:05:09,903 --> 00:05:17,194
the titan's body is filled with pressurized steam at three hundred and fifty degrees — a thruster built into a lightweight frame.

92
00:05:17,254 --> 00:05:19,245
So it isn't weak because it's light.

93
00:05:19,305 --> 00:05:20,898
It's fast because it's light.

94
00:05:20,958 --> 00:05:23,063
And the wall breaks because it's fast.

95
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:30,434
And none of this is a fictional invention.

96
00:05:30,494 --> 00:05:32,868
Real giants solved the identical problem.

97
00:05:32,928 --> 00:05:36,728
Argentinosaurus — thirty-five meters long, eighty tons of animal.

98
00:05:36,788 --> 00:05:39,340
Cut its bones open and you find them hollow.

99
00:05:39,400 --> 00:05:40,053
Like a bird,

100
00:05:40,113 --> 00:05:44,328
it packed air sacs deep into its skeleton to shed weight it didn't need.

101
00:05:44,388 --> 00:05:46,287
Keep the volume, cut the density.

102
00:05:46,347 --> 00:05:49,434
That is, precisely and exactly, the titan's solution.

103
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:02,153
But shedding weight is only half the job.

104
00:06:02,213 --> 00:06:04,475
You still have to hold up whatever is left.

105
00:06:04,535 --> 00:06:06,364
So look at a giant dinosaur's legs.

106
00:06:06,424 --> 00:06:07,228
They don't bend.

107
00:06:07,288 --> 00:06:10,143
They stand straight and vertical, like temple columns.

108
00:06:10,203 --> 00:06:12,680
A bent leg loads the joint with bending stress,

109
00:06:12,740 --> 00:06:14,408
and bending is what breaks bone.

110
00:06:14,468 --> 00:06:18,565
A straight one channels the force cleanly down the shaft and into the ground.

111
00:06:18,625 --> 00:06:22,830
It's the same reason an elephant's leg looks like a pillar instead of a spring.

112
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:26,357
Elephants went one step further than that.

113
00:06:26,417 --> 00:06:28,659
Compare a calf's leg bone to an adult's,

114
00:06:28,719 --> 00:06:33,379
and the adult's is disproportionately thicker for its body size — not just bigger,

115
00:06:33,439 --> 00:06:35,048
but bigger than it should be.

116
00:06:35,108 --> 00:06:37,810
Bone thickness was tuned to outpace body growth.

117
00:06:37,870 --> 00:06:40,112
Living things adjusted this dial slowly,

118
00:06:40,172 --> 00:06:42,529
over millions of years of trial and error.

119
00:06:42,589 --> 00:06:44,544
A fictional titan had no such time.

120
00:06:44,604 --> 00:06:46,961
So its author reached for density instead.

121
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:52,539
And then there's the heaviest animal in the history of this planet — the blue whale,

122
00:06:52,599 --> 00:06:54,072
one hundred and ninety tons.

123
00:06:54,132 --> 00:06:56,371
How does something that heavy move at all?

124
00:06:56,431 --> 00:06:56,919
The ocean.

125
00:06:56,979 --> 00:06:59,930
Underwater, buoyancy cancels gravity almost completely,

126
00:06:59,990 --> 00:07:04,584
and a blue whale never has to hold itself up on legs for a single second of its life.

127
00:07:04,644 --> 00:07:06,445
It didn't beat the curse of scale.

128
00:07:06,505 --> 00:07:08,800
It moved somewhere the curse doesn't apply.

129
00:07:08,860 --> 00:07:12,139
Put a titan in the sea, and physics wouldn't complain at all.

130
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:21,481
One piece is still missing.

131
00:07:21,541 --> 00:07:23,251
In the story, titans don't eat.

132
00:07:23,311 --> 00:07:26,961
They draw their energy from sunlight, and when the sun goes down,

133
00:07:27,021 --> 00:07:27,931
they simply stop.

134
00:07:27,991 --> 00:07:29,758
That isn't a coincidence either.

135
00:07:29,818 --> 00:07:35,580
Burning food for energy always produces heat — the exact problem we just watched kill them twice over.

136
00:07:35,640 --> 00:07:39,633
Switch the metabolism to sunlight, power the whole thing down at night,

137
00:07:39,693 --> 00:07:42,373
and the self-cooking problem quietly disappears.

138
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:46,872
So the titan's lightness was never a lazy shortcut.

139
00:07:46,932 --> 00:07:48,596
Bones that shatter under load.

140
00:07:48,656 --> 00:07:50,033
A body that cooks itself.

141
00:07:50,093 --> 00:07:52,907
And enough force to bring down a fifty-meter wall.

142
00:07:52,967 --> 00:07:56,241
One single answer solves all three at once: make it light.

143
00:07:56,301 --> 00:07:59,231
Good imagination doesn't ignore the laws of physics.

144
00:07:59,291 --> 00:08:03,427
It learns them first — and then goes looking for the gap to walk through.

